WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.686
Prorogation.

00:00:01.636 --> 00:00:04.750
Shutting down Parliament to avoid accountability.

00:00:04.750 --> 00:00:09.170
Prorogation is a constitutional mechanism that ends a parliamentary session.

00:00:09.170 --> 00:00:11.397
All committee business dies.

00:00:11.397 --> 00:00:13.636
All investigations stop.

00:00:13.636 --> 00:00:16.238
All pending legislation is killed.

00:00:16.238 --> 00:00:20.647
When used as a routine transition between sessions, it is unremarkable.

00:00:20.647 --> 00:00:29.181
When used to shut down active investigations — as in the Afghan detainee transfer and WE Charity scandals — it becomes a democratic escape hatch.

00:00:29.181 --> 00:00:31.318
How prorogation works.

00:00:31.318 --> 00:00:35.556
The Governor General prorogues Parliament on the advice of the Prime Minister.

00:00:35.556 --> 00:00:38.318
All committee business ends immediately.

00:00:38.318 --> 00:00:41.534
All pending legislation dies on the Order Paper.

00:00:41.534 --> 00:00:44.749
All investigations in progress are terminated.

00:00:44.749 --> 00:00:53.079
When Parliament resumes, committees must be reconstituted and investigations must start over from scratch — if the government allows them to restart at all.

00:00:53.079 --> 00:00:54.545
The pattern.

00:00:54.545 --> 00:01:05.284
When accountability institutions get too close — when committees are about to receive documents, when witnesses are about to testify, when non-confidence motions are pending — the PM shuts down Parliament.

00:01:05.284 --> 00:01:07.193
No vote required.

00:01:07.193 --> 00:01:11.590
No justification needed beyond the PM's request to the Governor General.

00:01:11.590 --> 00:01:18.329
The institution designed to hold the government accountable can be unilaterally silenced by the government it is supposed to hold accountable.

00:01:18.329 --> 00:01:21.840
This is the structural democratic deficit made literal.
